2D Structural Analysis — Help Guide
=====================================

HOW TO RUN THE PROGRAM
-----------------------
Run the analyser from the repository root:

    python -m structural_analysis.main <input_file>

Example:
    python -m structural_analysis.main inputs/portal_frame.txt


INPUT FILE FORMAT
-----------------
Input files are plain-text. Lines beginning with # are treated as comments
and are ignored. Sections can appear in any order. Each section starts with
a keyword followed (on the same line) by the number of data rows to read.

1. TITLE
   The next non-blank, non-comment line becomes the model title.

   Syntax:
       TITLE
       <description text>

   Example:
       TITLE
       Sample Portal Frame

2. NODES  <count>
   One row per node: id  x  y
   Coordinates are in metres.

   Syntax:
       NODES <n>
       <id>  <x>  <y>
       ...

   Example:
       NODES 4
       1  0.0  0.0
       2  0.0  3.0
       3  4.0  3.0
       4  4.0  0.0

3. MATERIALS  <count>
   One row per material/section: id  A  I  E
     A = cross-sectional area (m^2)
     I = second moment of area (m^4)
     E = Young's modulus (kN/m^2)

   Syntax:
       MATERIALS <n>
       <id>  <A>  <I>  <E>
       ...

   Example:
       MATERIALS 1
       1  0.02  0.08  200000.0

4. ELEMENTS  <count>
   One row per element: id  node_i  node_j  mat_id  [type]  [release]
     type    : FRAME (default) or TRUSS
     release : START, END, or BOTH — introduces an internal moment hinge
               at the start node, end node, or both nodes of the element.
               Omit this field if no release is needed.

   Syntax:
       ELEMENTS <n>
       <id>  <node_i>  <node_j>  <mat_id>  [FRAME|TRUSS]  [START|END|BOTH]
       ...

   Examples:
       ELEMENTS 3
       1  1  2  1  FRAME          # plain frame element
       2  2  3  1  FRAME  END     # moment released at end node
       3  3  4  1  TRUSS          # truss (axial-only) element

5. SUPPORTS  <count>
   One row per supported node: id  ux  uy  rz
     Each flag is 1 (restrained) or 0 (free).
     Pin:    1 1 0   (ux and uy fixed, rotation free)
     Roller: 0 1 0   (only vertical fixed)
     Fixed:  1 1 1   (all three DOFs fixed)

   Syntax:
       SUPPORTS <n>
       <node_id>  <ux>  <uy>  <rz>
       ...

   Example:
       SUPPORTS 2
       1  1  1  0    # pin support at node 1
       4  0  1  0    # roller support at node 4

6. LOADS  <count>  (nodal loads)
   One row per loaded node: id  fx  fy  mz
     fx  = horizontal force (kN), positive = rightward
     fy  = vertical force (kN), positive = upward
     mz  = applied moment (kN.m), positive = counter-clockwise

   Syntax:
       LOADS <n>
       <node_id>  <fx>  <fy>  <mz>
       ...

   Example:
       LOADS 2
       2  10.0  -10.0  0.0    # 10 kN right, 10 kN down at node 2
       3  10.0  -10.0  0.0

7. MEMBER_UDL  <count>  (uniformly distributed load along full element length)
   One row per loaded element: elem_id  wx  wy
     wx = load per unit length in the local x direction (kN/m)
     wy = load per unit length in the local y (transverse) direction (kN/m)
          positive wy acts in the positive local-y direction of the element.

   Syntax:
       MEMBER_UDL <n>
       <elem_id>  <wx>  <wy>
       ...

   Example:
       MEMBER_UDL 1
       1  0.0  -10.0    # 10 kN/m downward UDL on element 1

8. MEMBER_POINT_LOADS  <count>  (concentrated load at a point along an element)
   One row per load: elem_id  a  px  py
     a  = distance from start node (m)
     px = local-x component (kN)
     py = local-y (transverse) component (kN), positive = positive local-y

   Syntax:
       MEMBER_POINT_LOADS <n>
       <elem_id>  <a>  <px>  <py>
       ...

   Example:
       MEMBER_POINT_LOADS 1
       3  2.0  0.0  -15.0    # 15 kN downward at 2 m from start of elem 3


COMPLETE MINIMAL EXAMPLE
-------------------------
    TITLE
    Simple Cantilever Beam
    NODES 2
    1  0.0  0.0
    2  5.0  0.0
    MATERIALS 1
    1  0.02  0.08  200000.0
    ELEMENTS 1
    1  1  2  1  FRAME
    SUPPORTS 1
    1  1  1  1
    LOADS 1
    2  0.0  -20.0  0.0


EXPECTED OUTPUT (2D SOLUTION)
------------------------------
The program runs seven clearly labelled steps and prints results to the
terminal.

Step A — Input summary
  Confirms how many nodes, elements (frame / truss), materials, supports,
  and nodal loads were read. Notes any moment releases and member loads.

Step B — Equation numbering (E matrix and G vectors)
  Lists the global equation number assigned to each degree of freedom
  (Tx, Ty, Rz) at every node. Restrained DOFs are shown as 0 (they do
  not get an equation number).
  Also lists the G vector for each element, mapping its six local DOFs
  to global equation numbers.

  Example output:
    E matrix (equation numbers):
      Node      Tx      Ty      Rz
         1       0       0       1
         2       2       3       4
         3       5       6       7
         4       8       0       9

    G vectors:
    Elem 1 (1->2, frame): G = [0, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4]

Step C — Global stiffness matrix K and load vector F
  Prints the assembled K matrix (shown only when total DOFs <= 15;
  otherwise a size note is given).
  Reports the K symmetry check: max|K - K^T| should be near zero.
  Lists every entry of F with its DOF label and value (kN or kN.m).

Step D — Nodal displacements
  Reports the solve residual ||K_ff * D_f - F_f||, which should be near
  machine precision (~1e-12 or smaller) for a well-conditioned model.
  Prints a table of nodal displacements and rotations:

      Node          ux (m)          uy (m)        rz (rad)
         1    0.000000e+00    0.000000e+00   -1.145460e-02
         2    3.127718e-02    5.451075e-04   -8.036203e-03

Step E — Member end forces (local coordinates)
  Prints a table of the six end-force components for every element in
  the element's own local coordinate system:

      Elem  Type       N_i       V_i       M_i       N_j       V_j       M_j

    i = start node, j = end node.
    N = axial force (kN)     — positive is tension
    V = shear force (kN)
    M = bending moment (kN.m)

Step F — Support reactions and global equilibrium
  Prints a table of reactions at every restrained node:

      Node     Rx (kN)     Ry (kN)   Mz (kN.m)

  Reports the maximum nodal equilibrium residual (should be near zero).
  Reports global SFx and SFy (both should be near zero for a valid solution).

Step G — Storage report
  Compares dense vs. banded storage element counts and the estimated
  bandwidth saving from the current node numbering order.

  Example:
    Dense:  144 elements (12x12)
    Banded:  108 elements (hbw = 8), savings = 25%


ERROR CONDITIONS
----------------
If the stiffness matrix is singular the solver reports:
    *** ANALYSIS FAILED: Singular stiffness matrix. ***

Common causes:
  - Mechanism: e.g. a portal frame on two roller supports has no
    horizontal restraint and will sway freely.
  - Floating sub-structure: one or more nodes have no support and are
    not connected to any supported component.
  - Wrong element type: a pure truss model still needs sufficient pin
    supports to prevent rigid-body motion.

Warnings about disconnected components or mechanisms are printed before
the solve step to help identify the problem.
